# 3. 🟣 L — Liskov Substitution Principle

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Objects of a child class should be replaceable with objects of the parent class without breaking the program.**

In simple words:

> If `B` is a subtype of `A`, we should be able to use `B` wherever `A` is expected without unexpected behavior.

---

## 🐦 Example

### ❌ Bad Design

```text
Bird
 └── Fly()
      ├── Eagle
      └── Penguin ❌
```

```csharp
public class Bird
{
    public virtual void Fly()
    {
        // Fly
    }
}

public class Penguin : Bird
{
    public override void Fly()
    {
        throw new NotImplementedException();
    }
}
```

The problem is that a `Penguin` is a `Bird`, but it cannot fulfill the behavior expected from the parent class.

> [!CAUTION]
> If a child class has to **break, throw exceptions, or behave unexpectedly** when used as its parent, LSP is probably being violated.

---

### ✅ Better Design

```text
Bird
 ├── Eagle
 └── Penguin

IFlyable
 └── Eagle
```

```csharp
public class Bird
{
}

public interface IFlyable
{
    void Fly();
}

public class Eagle : Bird, IFlyable
{
    public void Fly()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Eagle is flying");
    }
}

public class Penguin : Bird
{
}
```

Now:

* `Eagle` → Bird + Flyable
* `Penguin` → Bird
* Only birds that can fly implement `IFlyable`

> [!TIP]
>
> ### 🧠 Mental Model
>
> **Don't force a child class to support behavior that doesn't make sense for it.**

---

# 4. 🟠 I — Interface Segregation Principle

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **A class should not be forced to implement methods that it does not need.**

Instead of creating one large interface, prefer **small and focused interfaces**.

---

## ❌ Bad Example

```csharp
public interface IWorker
{
    void Work();
    void Eat();
    void Sleep();
}
```

Now a robot is forced to implement methods it doesn't need:

```csharp
public class Robot : IWorker
{
    public void Work()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Robot is working");
    }

    public void Eat()
    {
        // Robot doesn't eat
    }

    public void Sleep()
    {
        // Robot doesn't sleep
    }
}
```

> [!WARNING]
> A class should not have to implement **meaningless or unused methods** just because an interface requires them.

---

### ✅ Better Design

Split the interface:

```csharp
public interface IWorkable
{
    void Work();
}

public interface IEatable
{
    void Eat();
}

public interface ISleepable
{
    void Sleep();
}
```

Human:

```csharp
public class Human : IWorkable, IEatable, ISleepable
{
    public void Work()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Human is working");
    }

    public void Eat()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Human is eating");
    }

    public void Sleep()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Human is sleeping");
    }
}
```

Robot:

```csharp
public class Robot : IWorkable
{
    public void Work()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Robot is working");
    }
}
```

> [!TIP]
>
> ### 🧠 Mental Model
>
> ❌ **One huge interface**
> ↓
> Classes implement things they don't need
>
> ✅ **Small interfaces**
> ↓
> Classes implement only what they need

