# 5. 🔴 D — Dependency Inversion Principle

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **High-level modules should not depend directly on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions.**

The simple version:

> **Depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.**

---

## ❌ Without DIP

```csharp
public class EmailService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending email...");
    }
}

public class OrderService
{
    private EmailService _emailService = new EmailService();

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // Place order

        _emailService.Send();
    }
}
```

`OrderService` is directly dependent on `EmailService`.

If tomorrow we want to send an SMS instead of an email, we need to modify `OrderService`.

---

## ✅ Using DIP

Create an abstraction:

```csharp
public interface INotificationService
{
    void Send();
}
```

Email implementation:

```csharp
public class EmailService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending email...");
    }
}
```

SMS implementation:

```csharp
public class SmsService : INotificationService
{
    public void Send()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Sending SMS...");
    }
}
```

Now `OrderService` depends on the abstraction:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;

    public OrderService(INotificationService notificationService)
    {
        _notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void PlaceOrder()
    {
        // Place order

        _notificationService.Send();
    }
}
```

### 🧠 Mental Model

```text
❌ DIP Violation

OrderService
      │
      ↓
EmailService
```

```text
✅ DIP

             INotificationService
                    ▲
                    │
             ┌──────┴──────┐
             │             │
          Email           SMS
             ▲             ▲
             └──────┬──────┘
                    │
              OrderService
```

> [!TIP]
> **DIP = High-level code should depend on abstractions, not implementation details.**

---

# 🔗 DIP vs DI

> [!NOTE]
> **DIP and DI are related, but they are NOT the same thing.**

### DIP — Dependency Inversion Principle

DIP is a **design principle**.

It tells us:

```text
Depend on abstractions
        ↓
Not concrete implementations
```

Example:

```csharp
private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;
```

---

### DI — Dependency Injection

DI is a **technique/pattern** used to provide dependencies from outside a class.

Instead of:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private EmailService _emailService =
        new EmailService();
}
```

We inject the dependency:

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;

    public OrderService(
        INotificationService notificationService)
    {
        _notificationService = notificationService;
    }
}
```

> [!TIP]
>
> ### 🧠 Easy Way to Remember
>
> **DIP → What should we depend on?**
> **DI → How do we provide that dependency?**

---

# ⚙️ .NET — Dependency Injection

> [!NOTE]
> **Dependency Injection is built into ASP.NET Core.**

Dependency Injection allows a class to receive its dependencies from outside instead of creating them itself.

### Benefits

* ✅ Loose coupling
* ✅ Better testability
* ✅ Easier maintenance
* ✅ Easier replacement of implementations
* ✅ Better separation of responsibilities

---

## ❌ Without Dependency Injection

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly EmailService _emailService;

    public OrderService()
    {
        _emailService = new EmailService();
    }
}
```

`OrderService` creates its own dependency.

This creates **tight coupling**.

---

## ✅ With Dependency Injection

```csharp
public class OrderService
{
    private readonly INotificationService _notificationService;

    public OrderService(
        INotificationService notificationService)
    {
        _notificationService = notificationService;
    }
}
```

The dependency is provided from outside.

---

# 🏗️ Dependency Injection in ASP.NET Core

Register the service:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();
```

Then inject it into the controller:

```csharp
public class OrderController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IOrderService _orderService;

    public OrderController(IOrderService orderService)
    {
        _orderService = orderService;
    }
}
```

ASP.NET Core's DI container creates the required implementation and provides it to the controller.

---

# ♻️ DI Service Lifetimes

There are three common service lifetimes in .NET:

|     Lifetime     | Meaning                                          |
| :--------------: | ------------------------------------------------ |
| 🟢 **Transient** | New instance every time the service is requested |
|   🔵 **Scoped**  | One instance per request/scope                   |
| 🔴 **Singleton** | One shared instance for the application lifetime |

---

## 🟢 1. Transient

```csharp
builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailService, EmailService>();
```

A **new instance** is created every time the service is requested.

> [!TIP]
> **Transient → Need a fresh instance every time?**

```text
Request
  ↓
New Instance

Request
  ↓
New Instance

Request
  ↓
New Instance
```

---

# 🔵 2. Scoped

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();
```

One instance is created for each **scope**.

In a typical ASP.NET Core application, the scope is usually an HTTP request.

```text
HTTP Request 1
     │
     ├── OrderService → Instance A
     ├── OrderService → Instance A
     └── OrderService → Instance A

HTTP Request 2
     │
     └── OrderService → Instance B
```

> [!TIP]
> **Scoped → One instance per request/scope.**

Scoped is commonly used for services that work with `DbContext`.

---

# 🔴 3. Singleton

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ICacheService, CacheService>();
```

Only one instance is created and shared throughout the application's lifetime.

```text
                Application
                     │
              Singleton Service
                     │
          ┌──────────┼──────────┐
          ↓          ↓          ↓
       Request    Request    Request
```

> [!WARNING]
> Singleton services are shared across requests, so they should generally be **thread-safe**.

> [!TIP]
> **Singleton → One shared instance for the application lifetime.**

---

# 🧠 DI Lifetime — Easy Revision

```text
┌──────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐
│ Transient    │ New instance every time    │
├──────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│ Scoped       │ One instance per request   │
├──────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤
│ Singleton    │ One for application       │
└──────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
>
> ### Easy Memory Trick
>
> 🟢 **Transient** → Every time
> 🔵 **Scoped** → Every request
> 🔴 **Singleton** → Entire application

